audio: move the I2S side to hart0, wire rate 44100

Copy esp32s31-audio.c over linux-71-port/sound/drivers/ and
esp32s31_generic.dts over linux-71-port/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/
before building. A DTS change needs BOTH `make linux` AND `make opensbi`.

FOUR THINGS MUST MATCH OR THERE IS NO SOUND AT ALL
  1. bootloader/sdkconfig: CONFIG_S31_AUDIO_ENABLE=y and
     CONFIG_S31_AUDIO_HOST_CODEC=y
  2. this dts: i2s0 disabled, freertos_audio okay, and the card's cpu dai
     pointed at &freertos_audio
  3. shared/s31_audio_sram.h: S31_AUDIO_HW_RATE (both sides derive from it)
  4. all three flashed together: loader, kernel, opensbi

The codec's MCLK/BCLK/WS/data pads can be driven by exactly one controller,
so the ASoC path and the hart0 path are mutually exclusive.

WHO OWNS WHAT
hart0 owns I2S, the mixer, the ASRC lanes and the shared rings. LINUX KEEPS
THE CODEC: the ES8389 is on an I2C bus Linux owns, shared with the touch
controller, and only Linux has a driver for the part (the loader's audio core
drives an ES8311, which this board does not have). CONFIG_S31_AUDIO_HOST_CODEC
is exactly that split and the Kconfig help was written for this board. Keeping
the codec on the Linux side is also what keeps the ALSA mixer controls - and
so s31route's sink switching and lvdesk's stored volume - where they were.

WIRE RATE 44100, NOT 48000
The design needs ONE fixed clock domain so a Linux stream setup cannot retune
I2S under another producer. The VALUE is a free choice, and 44100 is what this
board's content uses: s31-bt's A2DP route is ROUTE_RATE 44100, and the ES8389
takes 44100 and 48000 through the same ratio-256 coefficient row. At 44100 the
common case is bit-exact and a sink switch never changes rate under a running
application. I2S_STD_CLK_DEFAULT_CONFIG derives the dividers from the same
constant.

THE PIN MAP IS THE BOARD'S, NOT THE ES8311 DEFAULTS
MCLK unrouted (-1), BCLK 3, WS 4, DOUT 5, DIN 6, PA 7 - taken from the i2s0
pinctrl the ASoC path used. The Kconfig defaults are 52/53/55/56/54 and clock
into unconnected pads. hart0's DMA byte counter still advances in that state,
so the counter is NOT evidence of sound; this board's rule is that audio is
verified by ear.

VERIFIED
- music.wav at 44100 through "default" (plug -> s31route -> hw): 1,363,968
  bytes, zero XRUNs, zero underruns, and confirmed correct BY EAR.
- capture at 44100 works (529,244 bytes in 3 s).
- lvdesk's stored volume still restores (/etc/lvdesk/state, volume=61).

OPEN DEFECT - RATE CONVERSION DOES NOT WORK
Only 44100 plays. 8000, 11025, 22050 and 48000 all transfer ZERO bytes and
fail with "Unable to install hw params", and they fail identically through
plughw:0,0 with s31route out of the chain, so this is alsa-lib's plug layer
against this driver. NOT the cause, each ruled out by measurement: the ring
size (a 512-frame buffer at 11025 is 8 kB at the sink and still fails),
s31route's advertised period/rate/buffer constraints (changing all three moved
nothing and was reverted), and a missing resampler (libasound exports
snd_pcm_rate_open and _snd_pcm_rate_linear_open; built --with-pcm-plugins=all).
Cause unknown. It was invisible before because the ASoC path took 11025 and
22050 NATIVELY - see patches/0042 - so the converter was never exercised.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- 2026-09-13, the ASRC attempt ---

HOW RATE CONVERSION SHOULD BE FIXED: the ASRC lane, not software. hart0 owns
two lanes, s31_audio_asrc.c already drives them, and s31_audio.c already sizes
the input for the ratio and calls the lane for any stream whose rate is not
the wire rate. That is hardware conversion at zero CPU cost, and it takes
alsa-lib's plug layer out of the path entirely.

FIXED HERE: s31_audio_asrc.c hardcoded `.dest_info = { 48000, ... }`. That was
correct while the wire rate was 48000 and became silently wrong the moment it
moved to 44100 - the lane would convert every source to a rate the I2S no
longer runs at. It now uses S31_AUDIO_HW_RATE like everything else.

NOT ENABLED, AND WHY. Advertising the lane's rates from the Linux driver and
playing 11025 through it WEDGED THE BOARD: console silent, no panic, and hart0
came back clean on the next reset. The audio task is high priority and hart0
owns the radios, so a lane that never completes takes the whole board down.
s31_audio_asrc_convert() waits on the lane without a timeout.

THE ORDER OF WORK:
  1. Put a bounded wait on the lane in s31_audio_asrc_convert(), returning
     ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT so the caller's existing xrun-and-skip path handles it.
     The unbounded wait is what is unsafe, not the rate list.
  2. Re-measure the "808 xruns in four seconds" claim in esp32s31-audio.c.
     It was taken at the 48000 wire rate, before the pin map was corrected and
     before dest_info was fixed, so it is not evidence about the current
     configuration.
  3. Only then widen .rates in esp32s31-audio.c and supported_rate() in
     s31_audio_asrc.c together, and keep them in step.

SEPARATELY STILL OPEN: alsa-lib's plug cannot configure this device at all.
At 11025 it narrows to period 256 frames / 4 periods / a 4 kB buffer - 16 kB
at the sink, inside every limit - and still fails "Unable to install hw
params". The ranges it reports are degenerate (PERIOD_TIME (23219 23220) is an
open interval containing no integer, because 256 frames at 11025 is 23219.95
us), which suggests the driver's narrow period/buffer limits leave the
refinement nowhere to land. Fixing the ASRC path makes this moot for the rates
that matter.

--- 2026-09-13, the ASRC attempt, part two ---

THE LANE IS DISARMED AND MUST STAY SO UNTIL THE HANG IS UNDERSTOOD.
Arming it wedged the board TWICE, on the FIRST stream each time:

  attempt 1: rates widened, lane as shipped.
             Suspected an unbounded wait. IT IS NOT - the wait is
             xQueueReceive(..., pdMS_TO_TICKS(20)), bounded at 20 ms.
  attempt 2: same, after fixing a real descriptor leak (below).
             Wedged identically. So the leak was not the cause either.

Both times: console silent, hart0 clean on the next reset, no panic anywhere.
hart0 owns the radios and the console chatter, so when it goes there is no
post-mortem to read - which is exactly why the next attempt needs hart0
instrumented FIRST, not another blind variant. Two blind variants were already
tried and both cost a wedge.

KEPT, because they are correct independently of the lane:
  * .dest_info now derives from S31_AUDIO_HW_RATE instead of a hardcoded
    48000, which became wrong the moment the wire rate moved to 44100.
  * The two GDMA descriptor lists are created ONCE per lane in
    s31_audio_asrc_init() and only re-mounted per conversion. They were
    created on every call and never freed - asrc_hw_gdma_free_link_list()
    exists and was never used - which at ~172 blocks a second leaked two
    lists a block out of hart0's small heap. A real bug, just not this one.

ALSO LEARNED: after a wedge, a flash-and-reset is not enough to get sound
back - 44100 kept failing with "write error: I/O error" until a full
alive.py --reset, after which it worked first time. Do not diagnose audio on
a board that has just been wedged.
